WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.960
In January 2008, unbeknownst to the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.720
something shady was happening off&nbsp;
the southern coast of Sri Lanka.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.920
Construction was beginning on a shiny, new&nbsp;
4,000-acre port in the tiny town of Hambantota.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:23.280
85% of the $361 million price tag&nbsp;
was paid for with Chinese loans.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.720
But this, on its own, was no&nbsp;
cause for concern. China’s EXIM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.440
bank funds thousands of projects around the world.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.200
What was strange was the location.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.920
Positioned between the Suez Canal and Strait&nbsp;
of Malacca, tens of thousands of ships pass by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.200
each year on their way between Europe and Asia&nbsp;
— one of the busiest transcontinental routes.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.680
Yet, Sri Lanka, with a population of&nbsp;
21 million, already had a profitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:01.120
functioning port just 100-miles northwest, in&nbsp;
Colombo — the world’s 25th busiest, in fact.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.800
So why, then, build a second so close, in&nbsp;
such a small town, and at such great expense?

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.760
Sure enough, this skepticism&nbsp;
would soon be vindicated.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.880
In 2012, 3,667 ships berthed at&nbsp;
the neighboring port of Colombo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.160
Two years had passed then since the opening&nbsp;
of Hambantota, yet it attracted just… 34.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.400
By 2016, the project had lost&nbsp;
$230 million according to its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.080
ministry of finance.
And there’s more…

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.200
In addition to the sea port, an equally pristine&nbsp;
and equally empty airport was built nearby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.400
The last remaining airline pulled out&nbsp;
in 2018 due to insufficient demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.640
Its long runway and modern terminal&nbsp;
may now be used for… long-term parking.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.480
So, if not profit, what motivated&nbsp;
these extravagant investments?

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.080
It all begins to crystallize&nbsp;
when you just zoom out.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.520
Sri Lanka is geographically blessed not only&nbsp;
with proximity to global shipping lanes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:19.840
it’s also just 34-miles, at its closest point,&nbsp;
to India — one of China’s closest rivals.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.520
Coincidence? Maybe. But then, in 2017,&nbsp;
came what many see as the smoking gun.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.760
Unable to pay back its many&nbsp;
multi-million dollar loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.520
Sri Lanka was forced to hand over the&nbsp;
port and 15,000-acres around it, to China.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.320
It didn’t take a wild imagination to see how the&nbsp;
country then already notorious for making illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.240
claims in the South China Sea might use this&nbsp;
strategic outpost for more than mere shipping.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.080
Since then, close observers have watched the same&nbsp;
early warning signs play out around the globe.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.640
Tajikistan. Djibouti. Ethiopia. Kyrgyzstan.&nbsp;
Angola. Nigeria. What could once be dismissed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.440
as circumstantial, many now argue&nbsp;
has become a neocolonial playbook.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.840
It’s called [ ].

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.640
Here’s what happens:

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.880
First, China approaches a small, impoverished&nbsp;
nation — usually in Africa — with an offer it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:33.120
can’t refuse. Stadiums, palaces, roads, ports —&nbsp;
it doesn’t really matter what “it” is. Between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:40.560
2000 and 2019, Chinese banks have loaned an&nbsp;
estimated $153 billion to African governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.720
alone. Looking at a map, it’s hard to find a place&nbsp;
on the continent that hasn’t taken Chinese loans.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.440
Inevitably, the project fails. Trains&nbsp;
sit empty, airports open without flights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.000
roads lead to nowhere, and an oil&nbsp;
refinery runs at just 6% capacity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.320
In fact, it almost seems like&nbsp;
China wants them to fail.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.040
Finally, the coup de grâce. After the fanfare&nbsp;
has subsided and the recipient country is left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.400
right back where it started plus crumbling&nbsp;
roads and mountains of debt, in swoops China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:22.560
It generously offers to forgive the loans. And&nbsp;
all it asks for is one thing in return: a tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:29.120
insignificant piece of its sovereign territory.&nbsp;
And voilà — China has a new military base.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.840
Or, rather, that’s how the story goes.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.120
The “debt-trap” narrative is so often&nbsp;
repeated that it’s been labeled a “meme”.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.960
Yet almost everything said thus far is either&nbsp;
downright wrong or at least misleading.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.360
In the case of Sri Lanka, no debt was ever&nbsp;
forgiven, no sovereignty was ceded to China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.680
there is no military base, and China didn’t&nbsp;
approach Sri Lanka — Sri Lanka approached China.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.760
There’s another way to make&nbsp;
sense of China’s actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:05.920
And it neither requires that you&nbsp;
assume it’s evil, nor benevolent.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.480
Sponsored by Brilliant. Learn math, science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.760
and computer science the intuitive&nbsp;
way with the link in the description.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.880
When poor countries need money,&nbsp;
wealthy ones, through organizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.840
like the Development Assistance Committee&nbsp;
have, for the last 80 years, provided it.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.640
But what these largely-Western countries and banks&nbsp;
will pay for has changed dramatically over time.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.000
At one point, 70% of World Bank&nbsp;
financing went toward economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.520
infrastructure — physical things like&nbsp;
roads, water, and electricity. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.520
just 30%, in favor of things like education,&nbsp;
democratic elections, and family planning.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:54.240
But while the West has largely moved on from&nbsp;
infrastructure, the developing world hasn’t.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:02.160
Africa, for instance, receives only about&nbsp;
half of the $130-170 billion in infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:08.240
it needs each year, leaving it&nbsp;
with a massive $68-108 billion gap.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.520
Now, hold that thought for a second.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.600
Sometime around 2010, the world’s most populous&nbsp;
country had a problem. After two decades of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.120
breakneck growth and development, it began, to&nbsp;
put it simply, running out of things to build.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.240
China was still developing like&nbsp;
crazy, but with diminishing returns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.840
By 2012, the profit rate of new domestic&nbsp;
infrastructure projects fell below zero.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.960
Meanwhile, the entire economy, and&nbsp;
with it, the party’s legitimacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.240
depends on these construction jobs, excess&nbsp;
foreign exchange, and the manufacturing industry.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.200
In short: to feed the bottomless appetite of&nbsp;
Capitalism, China needed to find new consumers.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.360
Supply, meet demand. In Africa,&nbsp;
it found the perfect match.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.360
If one views development as a&nbsp;
predictable, repeatable series of stages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.920
China was, only recently, one stage&nbsp;
ahead of Africa — meaning it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.840
lots of experience building exactly&nbsp;
the sorts of things it now needed.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.960
Whether true or not, China believed&nbsp;
its port cities and economic zones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.840
were models that could be&nbsp;
exported across the globe.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.000
It was the perfect collision of excess supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.320
and unmet demand. In just a few short&nbsp;
years, Chinese overseas investment exploded.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:40.560
For recipient nations, this wasn’t just a lot of&nbsp;
money at the right time, but also the right kind.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.520
When giant, multilateral institutions&nbsp;
like the World Bank lend you money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.000
you better be prepared to show exactly how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.600
when, who, and where it went. And, in addition&nbsp;
to all the paperwork you’ll have to fill out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.560
and kickbacks you’ll miss out on, you might&nbsp;
even be required to implement policy reforms.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.680
Aid, in other words, is both&nbsp;
“free” and extremely costly.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.080
That is, until China came along. The icing&nbsp;
on the cake of Chinese money is that its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.560
companies don’t ask ‘Why do you need this&nbsp;
project?’, only ‘When can we get started?’

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.200
The result is that these investments&nbsp;
are designed to be as scrupulous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.560
and economically viable as&nbsp;
are the recipient governments.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:28.560
When local politicians are careful and&nbsp;
institutions strong, Chinese investment is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:34.080
uniquely valuable because it takes on countries&nbsp;
and projects someone more cautious might avoid.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:40.000
The Chinese shipping conglomerate Cosco, for&nbsp;
instance, turned Greece’s Piraeus Port into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:45.840
the Mediterranean’s second-largest, despite being&nbsp;
one of only two bidders to take control in 2016.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.480
Low-interest Chinese loans helped Angola boost its&nbsp;
credit rating, giving it access to new lenders.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.680
When it goes poorly, on the other&nbsp;
hand, it can go really poorly.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:03.920
The state-owned China Communications Construction&nbsp;
Company was debarred by the World Bank for bribery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.840
and Chinese officials were involved in&nbsp;
Malaysia’s Development Berhad Scandal.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:14.000
In the case of Sri Lanka’s Hambantota&nbsp;
port, the story is a bit more complex.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.480
First, you may ask: Was the&nbsp;
project destined to fail?

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.840
The answer is… inconclusive.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.560
What we do know is that its government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:28.160
had been considering the project for&nbsp;
decades before it approached China.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:32.080
And while the port of Colombo is indeed nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.480
it was then approaching capacity, and many of&nbsp;
the world’s ports are close to one another.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.600
A second and separate question&nbsp;
is: Was corruption involved?

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.120
Here the answer is a resounding… ‘yes’.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:51.520
Hambantota is not just any rural coastal&nbsp;
town, but the hometown of the president who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:58.000
signed off on the project. Despite a large rock&nbsp;
blocking the harbor, making it entirely unusable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.840
it “opened” on his birthday in 2010 — a sign&nbsp;
of his personal association and involvement.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.640
$7.6 million of project funds were secretly&nbsp;
diverted to his failed re-election campaign.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.600
If there is a “pattern” to&nbsp;
China’s overseas investments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.960
it might be frequent — though&nbsp;
not universal — corruption.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:24.080
Bribes and kickbacks are widespread&nbsp;
across much of the developing world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:27.360
and Chinese companies seem&nbsp;
generally willing to pay them.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.360
A significant number of&nbsp;
these projects benefit no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.400
except local politicians and Chinese developers.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:42.240
But corruption is not the same as a deliberate&nbsp;
debt-trap. If China wanted its borrowers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.840
default on their loans, one might expect it to&nbsp;
specifically target countries likely to do so.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:53.600
Yet, independent researcher after researcher&nbsp;
has failed to find any such evidence.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.920
Quote, “It is unlikely that [the] Belt&nbsp;
and Road Initiative will be plagued with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:02.400
wide-scale debt sustainability problems”,&nbsp;
says the Center for Global Development.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.680
The Lowy Institute finds that, quote,&nbsp;
“90% of China’s bilateral loans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.720
gone to countries that… could&nbsp;
sustainably absorb such debt”.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.720
And finally, Deborah Brautigam of Johns Hopkins&nbsp;
writes, quote, “so far, in Africa, we have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.440
seen any examples where we would say the Chinese&nbsp;
deliberately entangled another country in debt…”

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.360
Experts attribute China’s slightly higher&nbsp;
lending to high-risk countries as compared to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.640
say, Japan or the World Bank, to its sheer scale.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:37.440
Remember, we’re talking about over ten thousand&nbsp;
projects across over 100 countries. With this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:44.000
much money being spread across this wide a surface&nbsp;
area, there will be a few white elephants. Add in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.240
its “no-strings-attached”, “look-the-other-way”&nbsp;
approach, and that number doubles.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.720
Now, China may not set out with this intention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.880
but you might argue that once it sees&nbsp;
an opportunity, it takes advantage.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.480
Two countries are usually cited as examples.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:08.160
First, if all one knows about Sri Lanka is that it&nbsp;
took out a $300 million loan in 2008 from China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.200
who then took over that same port in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:14.800
it may appear to be a clear-cut&nbsp;
case of “debt-trap diplomacy”.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:21.280
But what this version of events misses is&nbsp;
context. At the time the deal was struck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.800
China was not even Sri Lanka’s largest source&nbsp;
of debt — actually it owed more to the Asian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:33.280
Development Bank, Japan, and the World Bank. The&nbsp;
Chinese portion represented just 10% of the total.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:37.920
So why did Sri Lanka make the deal?

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.600
In 2004, it suffered a devastating tsunami,&nbsp;
which contributed to a decline in exports.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:50.080
This, in turn, led to a “balance of trade”&nbsp;
problem. In short: because it was importing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:56.000
than it was exporting, it ran out of US Dollars,&nbsp;
which it needed to make payments on its loans.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:02.160
In other words, Sri Lanka needed hard&nbsp;
cash today, which China provided.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:07.840
It was almost certainly unwise to take out&nbsp;
even more loans to build the Hambantota&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:13.040
port — at least, at that time — but it&nbsp;
would have had this problem regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.440
because the vast majority of its debt was&nbsp;
owed to other nations and institutions.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:24.080
The exact details of the deal are also&nbsp;
frequently misconstrued. What actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:31.120
happened is that the Chinese company paid $1.1&nbsp;
billion for a 70% stake of a 99-year lease.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:35.360
No debt was forgiven in the&nbsp;
process — the money was used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:37.920
to pay off other loans from other countries.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:42.480
Neither will it gain a new&nbsp;
military base — at least, not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:48.080
as the lease stipulates, without&nbsp;
Sri Lanka’s explicit permission.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:49.280
Next is Djibouti.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:55.600
Here, in 2015, China built its first and&nbsp;
currently only overseas military base.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:01.680
But so has Germany. And Spain. And France.&nbsp;
The U.K.. Saudi Arabia. And the U.S..

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.760
Its colonial history is long and complicated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:10.080
But the ultra-condensed version is&nbsp;
that, in place of natural resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:17.520
Djibouti has access. Access to this 18-mile wide&nbsp;
chokepoint between the Red Sea and Indian Ocean.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:22.160
Its leaders have squeezed this fact&nbsp;
for all its worth by renting land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.640
to almost any military willing&nbsp;
to pay. Even Russia is invited.

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:34.080
Africa is not a country. Every nation&nbsp;
that borrows from China is different.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:39.520
The “debt-trap” narrative is right to&nbsp;
identify an inherent power differential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:43.840
between giant China and the (mostly)&nbsp;
much smaller countries it lends to.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:50.480
But, by portraying China as the only party with&nbsp;
agency, it neglects the unique circumstances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:55.280
of each nation and absolves corrupt&nbsp;
local politicians of all culpability.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:01.280
Take a look, for instance, at the amount&nbsp;
of debt China has refinanced, renegotiated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:07.600
or outright forgiven — less than France or&nbsp;
Japan, but not far behind the U.S. or Germany.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:12.160
This data suggests recipients&nbsp;
have some degree of leverage.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:16.240
Of course, none of this should&nbsp;
be confused with charity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:22.720
When Chairman Xi Jinping visits Senegal, one of&nbsp;
the continent’s poorest nations, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.840
China gains something valuable though intangible.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:32.480
While it may not be a primary motivation, it&nbsp;
receives, in return, diplomatic recognition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:37.760
support, and, when it comes to, say, the&nbsp;
South China Sea or Xinjiang, silence.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.680
So, is Chinese investment:

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:45.600
(A) An ambitious, 21st-century&nbsp;
Marshall Plan on steroids,

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:47.040
Or

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.360
(B) One, giant, trojan horse designed&nbsp;
to bankrupt poor countries and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.480
the People’s Liberation Army&nbsp;
in every corner of the globe?

00:15:55.760 --> 00:16:00.160
Well, neither; it’s not much of a ‘plan’ at all.

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:05.120
You may have noticed this video makes&nbsp;
no reference to its official name:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:06.800
“The Belt and Road Initiative”.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:10.800
That’s because… it doesn’t really exist.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:17.200
Originally called the “Silk Road Economic&nbsp;
Belt with Central Asian Countries” in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:22.160
the “project” has become increasingly&nbsp;
vague and amorphous since.

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:28.720
To this day, there is no clear definition of what&nbsp;
is and isn’t a Belt and Road project. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.040
different levels of its own government disagree.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:37.680
Look at a map of all “participating countries”&nbsp;
and it becomes so large as to lose all meaning.

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:45.120
There is no grand, cohesive, 50-year&nbsp;
strategy. And that’s the problem.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.480
The same inefficient construction and&nbsp;
opaque back-room deals that characterize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:54.880
the Chinese domestic economy&nbsp;
are now being replicated abroad.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:57.520
That should raise eyebrows.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:03.600
The danger of criticizing China for the wrong&nbsp;
reasons is that it reduces credibility when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:09.520
need it the most. That’s the importance of logical&nbsp;
consistency — a skill that today’s sponsor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:13.200
Brilliant teaches you in this&nbsp;
brand-new fun, interactive course.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:18.880
What I like about Brilliant is that it makes&nbsp;
learning fun by focusing on applying knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:23.840
